 This install applies to "4D Sports Driving" © 1992 Mindscape

 This install requires WHDLoad 17.0 or newer to be in the path. It can be found
 in the WHDLoad package.

 The installed program requires 0.5 MB ChipMem and 0.5 MB OtherMem.
 It can be quit by pressing num '*' (key can be changed with Tooltype "QuitKey=").

 The game requires an installed A500 Kickstart 1.3 image.
 The kickstart image must be located in the directory "Devs:Kickstarts" and
 must be named "kick34005.A500". The image can be extracted from a A500 with
 tools like 'GrabKick' or similar which can be found on aminet.
 If the image is of wrong version or altered in any way WHDLoad will quit with
 an appropriate requester.
 Additionally the file "kick34005.A500.RTB" must be installed in the same
 directory as the kickstart image. It can be found in the archive 
 "util/boot/skick346.lha" on aminet.


 If you have problems with this installation please try and get a newer
 version of this package from the WHDLoad WWW-Page. If that doesn't help try
 some Tooltypes like NOCACHE, NOAUTOVEC, NOVBRMOVE, NOMMU, PAL or NTSC. If
 that also fails, please inform the author via the Bug-Report-Form which
 can be accessed on the WHDLoad WWW-Page.

 For the full WHDLoad package, other installs and updates refer to:
	http://www.whdload.de/
 or	aminet:dev/misc/whdload#?
 and	aminet:game/patch/

 version 1.3b (13.10.2026):
 - splash simplified to one Enhanced framerate checkbox; credits now include
   Enhancements by Vesuri
 - one production slave includes corrected timing, optional rendering
   optimizations and experimental A4000 physics; no test fixtures
 - Normal runs at 10 simulation steps/s and A2500 at 20 steps/s, independent
   of rendering speed. The field clock detects PAL/NTSC and uses a 50/60
   phase accumulator. Slow rendering catches up the queued input in order.
 - without a saved choice, 68000/68010 select Normal; 68020 and later select
   A2500. CTRL-G changes detail and simulation settings. A changed simulation
   setting takes effect on the next race, and is saved in data/4DPhysics.cfg.
 - A4000 is an explicit opt-in, experimental 50 Hz mode for PAL machines.
   It updates physics and controls at 50 Hz without interpolation. It is
   never selected automatically by CPU type. On NTSC a saved A4000 choice
   falls back to the CPU default; A4000 selection/replay loading is blocked.
 - A4000 handling is not fully validated: 27/29 frozen handling checks pass.
   Common-Y travel is 2263 versus 1247, and landing pitch is 18 versus 11
   internal units in the two failing checks. Keep A4000 experimental.
 - A4000 replays use format 2, physics ABI9, with recorded opponent routes
   and state needed for deterministic playback. Older format-1 A4000 files
   are rejected. Native Normal/A2500 replay formats remain unchanged.
 - eligible 020+ machines with real Fast RAM can use a planar CPU renderer
   with Fast-RAM staging. Other configurations retain native drawing.
   The slave does not force extra CPU cache settings.
 - the splash checkbox "Enhanced framerate (68020+Fast for CPU renderer)"
   enables all rendering enhancements. Compatible optimizations also benefit
   68000 machines; the CPU renderer requires 68020+ and real Fast RAM.
   Corrected timing stays enabled; A4000 remains an in-game opt-in.
 - Advanced tooltypes (renderer-only and legacy timing are not splash options):
   CUSTOM2=0 (default): original rendering.
   CUSTOM2=1: all validated rendering optimizations, including the eligible
              Fast-RAM renderer and CPU-specific drawing paths.
   CUSTOM2=2: eligible Fast-RAM renderer only.
 - CUSTOM3=1: original simulation timing and original timing-hook bytes;
   Normal/A2500 remain available, A4000 replay loading is rejected.
   Rendering options remain independent of this timing switch.
 - memory above is the base requirement; WHDLoad, PRELOAD, replay storage
   and the optional renderer require additional host memory.

 Emulator measurements; real-hardware reports welcome.
 Ten-second PAL moving windows on the P6 production build. Version 1.3b
 changes only splash text/options and version metadata; game code is unchanged. Values are FPS,
 counted at display-buffer switches; FULL/FAST are graphics detail levels.
 CUSTOM2=0 and CUSTOM2=1 both use corrected timing (CUSTOM3=0).

 Machine / simulation           C2=0 FULL FAST    C2=1 FULL FAST
 A: 68000 / Normal 10 Hz              2.40 7.40         2.60 7.60
 B: 68EC020 / A2500 20 Hz             8.20 17.10       11.30 19.90
 C: 68030/50 / A2500 20 Hz           18.20 19.90       19.90 19.90
 D: 68040/25 / A2500 20 Hz           19.70 20.00       19.90 19.90
 E: 68060/50 / A2500 20 Hz           19.70 20.00       19.90 19.90

 A uses 512 KB Chip + 1.5 MB slow RAM; B/C use 2 MB Chip + 4 MB Fast;
 D/E use 2 MB Chip + 8 MB Z2 Fast + 8 MB motherboard RAM. C uses native
 IDE boot and active emulated data cache. D/E use calibrated CPU clocks;
 their data cache is not emulated in these directory-drive timing runs.
 These are scene/configuration-specific emulator results, not hardware
 predictions. The A500 FAST target of 9.5-10 FPS remains deferred.
 Queued steps crossing a window boundary can perturb short consumed-step
 rates; the clock funds 10/20 steps per second. No queue overflow occurred.

 Experimental A4000 50 Hz, same ten-second PAL method:
 Machine                       C2=0 FULL FAST    C2=1 FULL FAST
 D: 68040/25                         20.39 23.59       45.89 49.89
 E: 68060/50                         17.80 29.49       45.49 49.89

 Consumed simulation rate is 49.89-50.09 steps/s in these windows, with
 no queue overflow. The lower 060 result with original drawing illustrates
 why these emulator numbers must not be treated as hardware predictions.
 NTSC native 20 Hz also passes a 30-second check: 19.964 steps/s and
 race clock 0.9982 times emulated time. A4000 replay state on PAL matches
 all 1510 captured semantic bytes at steps 1500, 1550 and 1600.

 A separate full-68040 MMU functional test passes startup, 50 Hz gameplay,
 preference load/save with physical-file CRC verification, normal quit and
 return to the shell. WHDLoad's matching register dump confirms TC=8000,
 CACR=80008000 and Return OK. This is a compatibility test, not an FPS row.

 version 1.2 (21.03.2021) done by JOTD:
 - long delay when ending a race removed (side effect
   of music fix) (issue #0005034)
 - fixed graphical errors on displayed texts
   
 version 1.1 (09.09.2020)
 - music properly fixed (issue #004800)
 - enabled caches everywhere so Vampire and other non MMU
   users can play with caches on (better for 3D games!)
   
 version 1.0 (01.07.2006) done by JOTD:
 - adapted for whdload v16
 - music fixed (not perfect but works better)
 - package fully reworked
 - source code included

 version 1.0 (2001) done by JOTD:
 - initial release
 - SNOOP access fault removed
 - Full use of fastmem and caches for smooth 3D animation
 - Support for track expansion disk
 - password protection removed
 - Docfile included
 - Original quit menu still active
 - Nice icons by Exoticons
